import sys; sys.path.insert(0,'..')
from par_helpers import *
save(6,"The Teen Years, 13 to 18","Which of the qualities I admire in my teenager could I say aloud this week, and where in my home are they heard, valued and accepted?",[
mc("U","According to Lesson 6, why can a once-easy child seem to change so much in the teen years?",
 ["Something went wrong in the recent years at home, and the effects are only now showing.","Body and mind have matured, so the tendencies show, as a garden comes into season.","The child is rebelling against the family’s rules, as every young person must at this age.","New friends have taken over the child’s habits and have pushed the old ones aside."],1,
 "In the early years the body and mind are not yet developed enough for the saṁskāras to express themselves. With maturity they begin to, and the garden of the young child comes into season. It is no fault of the young person’s, and none of the parent’s. It is a personality unfolding.",
 "The garden comes into season: tendencies show as the body and mind mature","Part 1"),
mc("A","A seventeen-year-old slams her bedroom door after a sharp exchange with her mother. Which response fits Lesson 6?",
 ["Follow her in at once and talk it through while the matter is fresh.","Speak firmly through the door about the slam, so that it is not repeated.","Leave her alone until she has come out and has said she is sorry.","Wait a minute, then knock lightly and say, “I’m here when you want me.”"],3,
 "In that first minute the dial is resting on the state, so whatever is said then is said to the mood, not to her. When it has settled, knock lightly, say “I’m here when you want me,” and leave it there. A closed door is a young person finding a room of their own, not a verdict on the parent.",
 "Let the wave pass, then be available without pressing","Part 1"),
judge("“When a teenager argues with every request, it is a war against the parent.”",F,
 "What you see is two real things meeting: an inner freedom with new desires, and the structure of the home around it. The arguing is the “I” asserting what is innate in it, since freedom is the essence of the Self. It is not a war against you, and the parent’s part is to be a good observer and a good communicator.",
 "The push for freedom is the Self showing through a growing person","Part 1"),
mc("A","A parent is deciding whether to keep a rule from when her son was ten. Which test fits Lesson 6?",
 ["Take every rule down at once, since a teenager is now close to being an adult.","Ask of each rule, “Who is this for now?” and keep what protects, explained.","Keep every rule in place until he has shown for a year that he can manage without them.","Keep the rules he objects to least, as these are the ones he is most likely to accept."],1,
 "Not all at once and not blindly. Some fences still protect a young person, and those stay, explained. Others only protect a parent’s comfort, or the way things were done when the child was ten, and those can come down. A fence that guards safety is a different thing from one that guards habit.",
 "Ask of each fence, “Who is this one for now?”","Part 2"),
mc("U","According to Lesson 6, why do a teenager’s friends often reach them in ways a parent cannot?",
 ["Friends offer an easier route than a parent, who must also set rules.","The mind has changed, and it now drifts to those who resonate with it.","Friends are chosen largely by chance and then shape the young person’s tastes.","Young people look for those who will not question the habits they are forming."],1,
 "The friend is not the cause. Resonance is. In these years the mind itself is changing, and a changed mind finds new people, new ideas and new activities that resonate more strongly, and drifts towards them. A friend is where a young person recognises themselves.",
 "The heart goes where it resonates; the friend is not the cause","Part 2"),
mc("A","At a weekly family gathering a fifteen-year-old asks, “What is it even for?” Which reply fits Lesson 6?",
 ["“It is what our family does, and one day you will see the reason for it, I hope.”","“I’d like to hear what you think it’s for, then I’ll say what it means to me.”","“It matters to all of us, so please come, and you can ask me about it later.”","“If it means nothing to you, you can stay at home.”"],1,
 "Before the teens a child is open to the family’s decisions. In the teens the young person’s own values surface, and questioning is an attempt to bridge the gap. A parent might say: “I’d like to know what you think it’s for. And then I’ll tell you what it means to me.” Then she listens, and the young person is part of the system.",
 "Questions are bridges to belonging; answer with honest conversation","Part 3"),
mc("U","In Lesson 6, what is a teenager really doing when they ask, “Why do we do this?”",
 ["Challenging the parent’s authority, to find out how far it will hold for them.","Asking for a rule to be dropped without saying so directly to the parent.","Showing that the family’s ways no longer matter much to them at all.","Bridging the gap between their own mind and the family’s ways, to belong."],3,
 "Their questions express the distance between the mind they have inherited and the environment they live in. Seen that way, the young person is trying to align with what the family holds, not by imitation but by making it genuinely their own. It is not a challenge or a rebellion.",
 "Questioning is a way of integrating, not a rebellion","Part 3"),
mc("A","A parent sees that a fifteen-year-old’s low mood and withdrawal have lasted for weeks, and her sleep and schoolwork are now affected. Which response fits Lesson 6?",
 ["Wait, since the swings of these years settle once her own steering has grown.","Give her space and steady company at home, and trust that the mood will lift.","Speak to a doctor or counsellor without waiting; it is good parenting.","Teach her to say “Sadness is here,” and practise it with her each evening."],2,
 "Some ups and downs are part of these years. But if low mood, irritability or withdrawal lasts for weeks, or gets in the way of sleep, food, school or friends, or if there is any talk of harming themselves, please speak to a doctor or counsellor without waiting. That is part of good parenting, never a sign of failure.",
 "When low mood lasts for weeks or affects daily life, seek help without waiting","Part 4"),
judge("“Saying ‘Anger is here’ in place of ‘I am angry’ changes only the wording.”",F,
 "In “I am angry” the dial rests on the wave. In “Anger is here” it rests on the one who watches, the observer, the Self’s light showing through buddhi. The wave is still there, but one is standing a little way up the shore.",
 "“Anger is here”: the dial rests on the one who watches","Part 4"),
mc("A","A parent is troubled by the thorns she sees in her sixteen-year-old. Which approach fits Lesson 6?",
 ["Concentrate on the thorns first, since the flowers will look after themselves in time.","Accept what comes up, love the many good qualities, and keep the guidance and boundaries.","Wait, since the thorns will drop away of their own accord once he is a good deal older.","Say nothing of the thorns at all, so that he does not feel judged."],1,
 "A gardener cannot tend a plot she is angry with. Accept what comes up as the starting point, and love the many wonderful qualities you will see. Accepting is not approving: the guidance and the boundaries stay, and you tend without scolding the garden for being a garden.",
 "Thorns and flowers grow from the same garden; take pride in the whole person","Part 1")])
